# Prefer the `containers.podman` Collection

Do not shell out to the `podman` CLI via `ansible.builtin.command`,
`ansible.builtin.shell`, or `ansible.builtin.script` when an equivalent
`containers.podman` module exists. Use the collection module so behavior is
idempotent, `changed_when` is computed correctly, and output is
structured instead of parsed from text.

## Common mappings

| Instead of `podman ...` | Use |
| --- | --- |
| `build` / `pull` / `push` | `containers.podman.podman_image` |
| `tag` | `containers.podman.podman_tag` |
| `run` / `stop` / `rm` | `containers.podman.podman_container` |
| `login` | `containers.podman.podman_login` |
| `inspect` (container) | `containers.podman.podman_container_info` |
| `inspect` (image) | `containers.podman.podman_image_info` |
| `generate systemd` | `containers.podman.podman_container` with `state: quadlet` (see below) |

## Example

```yaml
# BAD
- name: Push the built image
  changed_when: true
  ansible.builtin.command:
    cmd: "podman push {{ app_image_registry }}/{{ app_image_name }}:{{ app_image_tag }}"

# GOOD
- name: Push the built image
  containers.podman.podman_image:
    name: "{{ app_image_registry }}/{{ app_image_name }}"
    tag: "{{ app_image_tag }}"
    push: true
```

## Quadlet, not `podman_generate_systemd`

`podman generate systemd` (and the `containers.podman.podman_generate_systemd`
module) is deprecated upstream and fails against a container that doesn't
already exist. Use `containers.podman.podman_container` with `state: quadlet`
to write a `.container` unit instead, then reload systemd so Podman's
generator turns it into a `.service` unit:

```yaml
- name: Write the Quadlet container unit
  containers.podman.podman_container:
    name: my-app
    image: quay.io/example/my-app:latest
    state: quadlet
    quadlet_dir: /etc/containers/systemd

- name: Reload systemd so the Quadlet generator picks up the unit
  ansible.builtin.systemd:
    daemon_reload: true

- name: Restart and enable the application service
  ansible.builtin.systemd:
    name: my-app.service
    state: restarted
    enabled: true
```

## Accepted exceptions

- `podman system migrate` has no collection module equivalent; `command` is
  fine for that specific subcommand.
- A temporary wrapper script invoked via a module's `executable` parameter
  is fine when a global flag (e.g. `--storage-opt`) has no module-level
  parameter, since the module itself is still doing the work.

Check the `containers.podman` module index before adding a new raw CLI
call -- new modules land there faster than this rule can enumerate them.
